{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROOT CAUSE CONFIRMED by reading src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py at origin/main ae6f2ac57a. The premise holds exactly as filed:\n\n1. _acquire_advisory_lock (lines 45-57) returns `os.open(path, O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_WRONLY, 0o644)` and NEVER writes to the returned fd. That is why the leaked lock on this host is 0 bytes: the file has no owner stamp because nothing ever writes one.\n2. The only `except FileExistsError` branch returns None immediately. There is NO staleness check, NO liveness probe, NO age bound, NO reclaim anywhere in the module — confirmed by grep for the lock name across src/: the only two sites are acquire and release.\n3. _release_advisory_lock (lines 60-65) is the SOLE unlink, called from drain()'s `finally`. A drainer killed with SIGKILL, OOM-reaped, or dying with the interpreter (the drain runs in a DETACHED child via _spawn_detached_drain, so it outlives its parent and is unsupervised) never runs that finally, and the file survives forever.\n4. drain() then logs the skip at logger.INFO, not WARNING, and returns {\"skipped\": \"lock-held\", \"processed\": 0} — the caller treats that as success, so the wedge is invisible.\n\nSo the failure is permanent and silent by construction, matching the observed ~26-day 0-byte lock with no drain process alive. No alternative explanation was found: nothing else in src/ creates, inspects, or removes this path, and neither the queue claim (lease-expiring, in overlap/queue.py) nor the store write lock covers it.\n\nSecondary finding worth recording: an EMPTY stamp is NOT reclaimable under lock_owner's current mkdir routing (`_parse_v2_stamp('')` -> None -> `_legacy_stamp_is_stale('')` -> no ':' -> False). So merely stamping new locks would leave every ALREADY-leaked 0-byte file wedged forever. The plan therefore routes 'unrecognised stamp' to the shared wall-clock ceiling for the drain lock (via an explicit `unrecognised_via_ceiling` parameter on the extracted core) while preserving the mkdir leg's legacy routing byte-for-byte.\n\nPlan PASSED review-plan (signed, head ae6f2ac57a) and the ticket is claimed. Implementing in worktree ../bluebottle-enrich-lock."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786597851429436001,"uuid":"ed0fb03d-adad-46c2-beef-e8cc6bff127b"}